sweet: the display reads iOS frames itself (full GPU scanout, no CPU copy)

Every frame backboardd swaps was copied by the CPU into the ABL's panel
memory (0x9c000000): 1125x2436 scaled to 1080x2340, ~11 MB read and ~10 MB
written per frame, the last CPU work left in the display path. Here the pipe
is pointed at the CoreAnimation IOSurface itself at every swap and VIG0's
QSEED3 scaler does the fit: the GPU renders, the display scans, the CPU
copies nothing.

This is the same SoC (SM8150), the same DPU 5.0, the same 1080x2340 panel and
1125x2436 canvas as the proven UniXNU-andromeda port (patch 0207 of
giannoccarol/UniXNU-andromeda, itself from ShueiTsuki's data-a-live 0217):
sweet_scanout.c (staged by 0268-stage-sweet-scanout.sh) installs the display
stream's own stage-1 tables in apps_smmu bank 2 (SID 0x800, SMR 3, the state
the 0263/0265 probe found on raphael: TRANSLATE, not BYPASS, so the old
sweetfb_zc refusal does not apply), maps the 36 MiB splash carve-out at its
own address so the copy fallback and the console still work, and puts
surfaces in 8 x 16 MiB IOVA slots from 0x80000000. The tables themselves live
in the carve-out's tail because every byte xnu manages is above 4 GiB and the
SMMU's 32-bit output size could not reach a kernel allocation.

Boot args: sweetfb_mdp=1 sweetfb_scanout=1 (plus sweetfb_scale=2 for the
1125->1080 stretch). Without sweetfb_scanout=1 nothing changes. The hardware
is set up at the first Metal frame and only if the pipe is exactly the ABL's
setup; any mismatch, SMMU fault or untaken flush leaves the CPU copy for the
boot (fail closed). A panic puts the pipe back on the panel memory.

